1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,700
Moving on to the thyroid gland,

2
00:00:01,940 --> 00:00:04,220
the first disorder is thyroglossal duct cyst.

3
00:00:04,300 --> 00:00:05,340
In order to understand this,

4
00:00:05,400 --> 00:00:07,720
you've got to remember that the thyroid develops

5
00:00:07,720 --> 00:00:08,740
at the base of the tongue,

6
00:00:09,080 --> 00:00:11,240
and then it travels along the thyroglossal duct

7
00:00:11,240 --> 00:00:12,300
to the anterior neck.

8
00:00:12,640 --> 00:00:15,120
Now, normally, this thyroglossal duct should involute.

9
00:00:15,320 --> 00:00:16,460
However, if it persists,

10
00:00:16,520 --> 00:00:19,100
you can then get cystic dilation of that duct,

11
00:00:19,160 --> 00:00:21,220
which would result in a thyroglossal duct cyst.

12
00:00:21,580 --> 00:00:24,820
And, of course, this would present as an anterior neck mass.

13
00:00:24,900 --> 00:00:26,060
It's particularly high-yield

14
00:00:26,060 --> 00:00:28,600
because it goes into a little bit of embryology.

15
00:00:28,600 --> 00:00:31,440
Lingual thyroid is when you have persistence of thyroid tissue

16
00:00:31,440 --> 00:00:32,380
at the base of the tongue,

17
00:00:32,520 --> 00:00:34,740
so you don't get that descent of the thyroid tissue

18
00:00:34,740 --> 00:00:35,740
to the anterior neck,

19
00:00:36,020 --> 00:00:38,220
and this would present as a base of tongue mass.

20
00:00:38,360 --> 00:00:39,920
And, again, that's particularly high-yield,

21
00:00:40,020 --> 00:00:41,400
again, tying into embryology.

22
00:00:42,860 --> 00:00:44,680
Hyperthyroidism is when you have an increased level

23
00:00:44,680 --> 00:00:46,400
of circulating thyroid hormone.

24
00:00:46,820 --> 00:00:48,940
This would increase the basal metabolic rate

25
00:00:48,940 --> 00:00:51,620
and also increase the sympathetic nervous system activity.

26
00:00:52,080 --> 00:00:53,040
Very high-yield to note.

27
00:00:53,160 --> 00:00:54,280
Board examiners like you to know

28
00:00:54,280 --> 00:00:56,440
how it increases the basal metabolic rate.

29
00:00:56,440 --> 00:01:00,580
It increases synthesis of sodium-potassium ATPase,

30
00:01:01,140 --> 00:01:03,580
whereas the increased sympathetic nervous system activity

31
00:01:03,580 --> 00:01:06,320
is due to expression of beta-1 adrenergic receptors.

32
00:01:06,500 --> 00:01:07,760
Again, both of these are high-yield,

33
00:01:07,880 --> 00:01:12,100
sodium-potassium ATPase and beta-1 adrenergic receptors.

34
00:01:12,600 --> 00:01:14,260
Now, the clinical features are going to be based

35
00:01:14,260 --> 00:01:15,700
on an increased BMR,

36
00:01:15,840 --> 00:01:17,120
so the way I think about this,

37
00:01:17,180 --> 00:01:18,440
the body goes into overdrive.

38
00:01:18,780 --> 00:01:21,500
You get weight loss despite increased appetite,

39
00:01:21,920 --> 00:01:23,400
heat intolerance and sweating.

40
00:01:23,400 --> 00:01:24,920
Again, you're burning a lot of ATP,

41
00:01:25,340 --> 00:01:26,440
which is going to generate heat.

42
00:01:26,960 --> 00:01:29,560
The patients get tachycardic with increased cardiac output.

43
00:01:29,740 --> 00:01:31,100
Again, they've got that increased

44
00:01:31,100 --> 00:01:32,640
sympathetic nervous system activity.

45
00:01:33,180 --> 00:01:34,680
Arrhythmias, for example, AFib,

46
00:01:34,740 --> 00:01:36,720
can also occur, especially in the elderly.

47
00:01:37,140 --> 00:01:39,140
They get tremor, anxiety, insomnia,

48
00:01:39,660 --> 00:01:43,000
heightened emotions, staring gaze with lid lag,

49
00:01:43,160 --> 00:01:44,520
diarrhea with malabsorption.

50
00:01:44,640 --> 00:01:46,600
Again, the idea here is that the gut's sort of moving

51
00:01:46,600 --> 00:01:47,820
a little bit faster as well.

52
00:01:48,120 --> 00:01:50,900
Patients with hyperthyroidism can develop oligomenorrhea.

53
00:01:50,900 --> 00:01:53,800
They get bone resorption with hypercalcemia.

54
00:01:53,960 --> 00:01:56,080
This could eventually lead to osteoporosis,

55
00:01:56,340 --> 00:01:58,820
and they get decreased muscle mass with weakness.

56
00:01:59,180 --> 00:02:01,820
Finally, the patients can get hypocholesterolemia.

57
00:02:02,260 --> 00:02:03,700
This is particularly high yield.

58
00:02:03,780 --> 00:02:06,760
The examiners like you to know that you get hypocholesterolemia

59
00:02:06,760 --> 00:02:09,800
with hyperthyroidism and hyperglycemia.

60
00:02:10,200 --> 00:02:12,140
And the idea here is that the thyroid hormone

61
00:02:12,140 --> 00:02:15,800
causes both gluconeogenesis and glycogenolysis,

62
00:02:15,940 --> 00:02:17,600
resulting in extra sugar in the blood.

63
00:02:17,600 --> 00:02:20,760
The most common cause of hyperthyroidism is Graves' disease.

64
00:02:20,880 --> 00:02:22,560
This is where you have an IgG antibody

65
00:02:22,560 --> 00:02:24,940
that stimulates the TSH receptor.

66
00:02:25,340 --> 00:02:27,020
When you stimulate the TSH receptor,

67
00:02:27,140 --> 00:02:29,000
you increase the production of thyroid hormone,

68
00:02:29,180 --> 00:02:31,920
and you also increase the release of thyroid hormone.

69
00:02:32,300 --> 00:02:33,520
So two things are going to occur,

70
00:02:33,620 --> 00:02:35,620
increased production plus increased release.

71
00:02:36,060 --> 00:02:37,980
This is the most common cause of hyperthyroidism,

72
00:02:38,020 --> 00:02:38,780
as I've already stated,

73
00:02:39,220 --> 00:02:41,440
and it classically occurs in women of childbearing age.

74
00:02:41,500 --> 00:02:43,040
Again, this is the classic demographic

75
00:02:43,040 --> 00:02:44,300
for an autoimmune disease.

76
00:02:44,820 --> 00:02:47,040
Clinically, the patients are going to have hyperthyroidism.

77
00:02:47,040 --> 00:02:48,280
Again, you have increased production

78
00:02:48,280 --> 00:02:50,400
and secretion of thyroid hormone.

79
00:02:50,800 --> 00:02:51,920
They get a diffuse goiter.

80
00:02:52,460 --> 00:02:55,060
As this antibody is stimulating the TSH receptor,

81
00:02:55,180 --> 00:02:57,380
that's going to cause massive hyperplasia

82
00:02:57,380 --> 00:03:00,140
and hypertrophy of the thyroid diffusely,

83
00:03:00,280 --> 00:03:02,080
resulting in a diffusely enlarged thyroid.

84
00:03:02,260 --> 00:03:04,700
And the word goiter refers to an enlarged thyroid.

85
00:03:05,200 --> 00:03:07,880
And they get epitophthalmos and pretibial myxedema.

86
00:03:08,400 --> 00:03:10,740
Now, this is very important to note and very high yield.

87
00:03:11,160 --> 00:03:13,200
This is not due to the hyperthyroidism.

88
00:03:13,200 --> 00:03:15,660
It is due to the fact that the patients have an antibody

89
00:03:15,660 --> 00:03:17,680
that stimulates the TSH receptor.

90
00:03:18,160 --> 00:03:20,820
So the idea here is that the fibroblasts behind the eye,

91
00:03:21,240 --> 00:03:23,160
as well as the fibroblasts over the shin,

92
00:03:23,300 --> 00:03:24,560
they have TSH receptors.

93
00:03:25,040 --> 00:03:27,040
And so in response to this antibody,

94
00:03:27,200 --> 00:03:30,540
they actually begin to secrete excess glycosaminoglycans,

95
00:03:30,860 --> 00:03:33,300
which then results in exophthalmos,

96
00:03:33,380 --> 00:03:35,020
because the eye gets pushed forward,

97
00:03:35,380 --> 00:03:36,980
and a pretibial myxedema.

98
00:03:37,380 --> 00:03:40,340
Edema here refers to the fact that it looks like

99
00:03:40,340 --> 00:03:41,360
there's swelling of the tissue,

100
00:03:41,680 --> 00:03:44,280
but the word mix refers to the fact that

101
00:03:44,280 --> 00:03:45,260
it's not due to water,

102
00:03:45,400 --> 00:03:47,260
but instead it's due to glycosaminoglycans,

103
00:03:47,340 --> 00:03:48,880
or a mixoidy substance.

104
00:03:49,400 --> 00:03:51,300
And clinically, they classically describe this

105
00:03:51,300 --> 00:03:52,640
as a doe-like appearance,

106
00:03:52,740 --> 00:03:54,120
so that when you press over the shin,

107
00:03:54,180 --> 00:03:57,500
you actually can feel a doe-like consistency to the tissue.

108
00:03:58,080 --> 00:03:59,580
Here's a picture of Graves' disease.

109
00:03:59,720 --> 00:04:01,500
You can clearly see that this is a goiter,

110
00:04:01,620 --> 00:04:02,400
an enlarged thyroid,

111
00:04:02,520 --> 00:04:04,420
and you can see that it's diffusely enlarged.

112
00:04:04,760 --> 00:04:06,180
This is a histologic image

113
00:04:06,180 --> 00:04:07,800
taken from a thyroid with Graves' disease,

114
00:04:07,800 --> 00:04:11,520
and you can clearly see that there's hyperplasia of the follicles.

115
00:04:11,640 --> 00:04:12,840
Notice how large they've become.

116
00:04:13,220 --> 00:04:14,720
One of the very high-yield findings here

117
00:04:14,720 --> 00:04:17,040
is that you also get scalloping of the colloid.

118
00:04:17,260 --> 00:04:18,420
In order to understand this,

119
00:04:18,500 --> 00:04:20,620
look at this follicle here that I'm outlining.

120
00:04:20,740 --> 00:04:21,460
This is normal.

121
00:04:22,000 --> 00:04:23,940
Notice that the colloid in this follicle,

122
00:04:24,040 --> 00:04:24,820
the pink material,

123
00:04:25,120 --> 00:04:27,680
is totally up against the blue epithelium

124
00:04:27,680 --> 00:04:29,900
with no really clear white space.

125
00:04:30,240 --> 00:04:32,980
However, notice that in this follicle here,

126
00:04:33,220 --> 00:04:35,020
you clearly have epithelium,

127
00:04:35,020 --> 00:04:37,040
but you've got this white space in between,

128
00:04:37,500 --> 00:04:40,560
and this white space kind of appears scalloped like this,

129
00:04:40,940 --> 00:04:43,180
and so they call that scalloping of the colloid,

130
00:04:43,540 --> 00:04:47,100
and that's a particularly classic finding in Graves' disease.

131
00:04:47,560 --> 00:04:48,040
Now, of course,

132
00:04:48,120 --> 00:04:50,580
these patients are going to be producing excess thyroid hormone,

133
00:04:50,960 --> 00:04:54,420
so there will be both an increase in total and free T4.

134
00:04:55,080 --> 00:04:56,640
The TSH will go down,

135
00:04:56,760 --> 00:04:58,520
and that's because the free T4

136
00:04:58,520 --> 00:05:01,020
will down-regulate thyroid-releasing hormone receptors

137
00:05:01,020 --> 00:05:02,340
in the anterior pituitary.

138
00:05:02,340 --> 00:05:05,520
Recall that the anterior pituitary

139
00:05:05,520 --> 00:05:08,560
secretes thyroid-stimulating hormone,

140
00:05:08,940 --> 00:05:12,980
but only in response to thyroid-releasing hormone

141
00:05:12,980 --> 00:05:14,940
that comes from the hypothalamus.

142
00:05:15,380 --> 00:05:16,540
And so what T4 does

143
00:05:16,540 --> 00:05:18,840
is that it down-regulates these receptors

144
00:05:18,840 --> 00:05:20,840
so that the anterior pituitary

145
00:05:20,840 --> 00:05:23,420
will then no longer be able to receive this signal,

146
00:05:23,760 --> 00:05:25,480
and therefore will produce less TSH.

147
00:05:26,280 --> 00:05:28,440
The patients will also have hypocholesterolemia.

148
00:05:28,680 --> 00:05:29,820
I've already explained that,

149
00:05:29,820 --> 00:05:31,980
and they'll have an increased serum glucose.

150
00:05:32,100 --> 00:05:33,500
I've already explained that as well.

151
00:05:34,300 --> 00:05:36,540
The treatment for Graves' disease includes beta blockers.

152
00:05:36,600 --> 00:05:38,400
Again, you're trying to block the effects

153
00:05:38,400 --> 00:05:40,080
on the sympathetic nervous system.

154
00:05:40,520 --> 00:05:41,740
Thyomite is another treatment.

155
00:05:42,160 --> 00:05:45,140
This is a drug that has the ability to block peroxidase.

156
00:05:45,740 --> 00:05:47,200
Let's go back and remind ourselves

157
00:05:47,200 --> 00:05:49,800
that this is the follicular cell of the thyroid.

158
00:05:49,800 --> 00:05:52,800
Remember that the cell takes tyrosine,

159
00:05:53,460 --> 00:05:56,320
and it converts tyrosine into thyroglobulin,

160
00:05:56,840 --> 00:05:58,920
and then the thyroglobulin is pushed off

161
00:05:58,920 --> 00:06:01,340
into the follicular lumen.

162
00:06:01,500 --> 00:06:02,820
This is where the colloid would be.

163
00:06:03,300 --> 00:06:04,260
And then at the same time,

164
00:06:04,320 --> 00:06:06,540
the cell takes iodide from the blood

165
00:06:06,540 --> 00:06:08,600
and brings it into the cell,

166
00:06:08,700 --> 00:06:10,800
and then crosses it over to the follicular lumen,

167
00:06:10,800 --> 00:06:14,660
where it undergoes oxidation and becomes I2.

168
00:06:15,260 --> 00:06:18,100
Remember that the thyroglobulin and the I2

169
00:06:18,100 --> 00:06:19,160
are then added together

170
00:06:19,160 --> 00:06:21,260
in a step called organification,

171
00:06:21,880 --> 00:06:24,080
and that's going to result in thyroglobulin

172
00:06:24,080 --> 00:06:30,880
with monoiodotyrosine and diiodotyrosine.

173
00:06:31,300 --> 00:06:31,840
And of course,

174
00:06:32,200 --> 00:06:34,980
the mono and the di will be coupled together,

175
00:06:34,980 --> 00:06:38,120
and that's going to then produce thyroglobulin

176
00:06:38,120 --> 00:06:41,800
with T3 when mono and di are coupled together,

177
00:06:41,800 --> 00:06:45,540
or T4 when the di and di are coupled together.

178
00:06:45,960 --> 00:06:48,520
Now, important to note that all of these steps here,

179
00:06:49,200 --> 00:06:52,920
the oxidation, the organification, and the coupling,

180
00:06:53,300 --> 00:06:55,480
these are all catalyzed by peroxidase.

181
00:06:56,020 --> 00:06:57,320
And so if you block peroxidase,

182
00:06:57,400 --> 00:06:58,780
obviously you're going to block the ability

183
00:06:58,780 --> 00:07:00,840
to produce a thyroid hormone.

184
00:07:01,420 --> 00:07:04,480
And so that's how thioamide works.

185
00:07:05,160 --> 00:07:07,600
Another possibility would be to use radioiodine

186
00:07:07,600 --> 00:07:09,240
to ablate the thyroid.

187
00:07:09,480 --> 00:07:11,340
The idea here is if you give radioiodine,

188
00:07:11,340 --> 00:07:12,800
of course, that would be taken up

189
00:07:12,800 --> 00:07:14,240
by the follicular cells of the thyroid,

190
00:07:14,600 --> 00:07:16,180
which would then result in destruction

191
00:07:16,180 --> 00:07:18,700
of the thyroid cells because of the radioactivity,

192
00:07:19,100 --> 00:07:21,580
and so you would ablate or destroy the thyroid gland.

193
00:07:22,020 --> 00:07:23,980
The thyroid storm is the feared complication

194
00:07:23,980 --> 00:07:24,840
of Graves' disease.

195
00:07:25,000 --> 00:07:26,760
This is when you have excess catecholamines

196
00:07:26,760 --> 00:07:27,900
and massive hormone,

197
00:07:28,380 --> 00:07:29,800
usually due to some sort of stress,

198
00:07:29,900 --> 00:07:31,400
like surgery or childbirth.

199
00:07:31,800 --> 00:07:33,760
The patients can present with arrhythmia,

200
00:07:33,900 --> 00:07:35,240
hyperthermia, and vomiting

201
00:07:35,240 --> 00:07:36,760
with a hypovolemic shock,

202
00:07:37,140 --> 00:07:40,000
and this is treated with PTU beta blockers and steroids.

203
00:07:40,000 --> 00:07:42,620
Recall from pharmacology, particularly high yield,

204
00:07:42,720 --> 00:07:45,640
that PTU inhibits peroxidase-mediated oxidation,

205
00:07:46,180 --> 00:07:47,720
organification, and coupling,

206
00:07:47,840 --> 00:07:48,820
as I've already explained.

207
00:07:49,200 --> 00:07:52,060
All three of those steps are also going to be inhibited by PTU.

208
00:07:52,480 --> 00:07:55,680
In addition, PTU inhibits the peripheral conversion

209
00:07:55,680 --> 00:07:57,020
of T4 to T3.

210
00:07:57,400 --> 00:07:59,080
Recall that the major hormone released

211
00:07:59,080 --> 00:08:00,720
by the thyroid gland is T4,

212
00:08:01,060 --> 00:08:03,680
and then T4 gets converted peripherally to T3,

213
00:08:03,780 --> 00:08:05,120
which is the more active form.

214
00:08:05,120 --> 00:08:08,180
And so PTU will also block the peripheral conversion

215
00:08:08,180 --> 00:08:09,720
of T4 to T3.

216
00:08:10,440 --> 00:08:12,920
Multinodular goiter is an enlarged thyroid gland

217
00:08:12,920 --> 00:08:14,340
with multiple nodules.

218
00:08:14,460 --> 00:08:16,680
Again, the goiter refers to an enlarged thyroid gland,

219
00:08:16,740 --> 00:08:19,460
and the multinodular means that you've got multiple nodules.

220
00:08:19,820 --> 00:08:21,600
It's due to a relative iodine deficiency.

221
00:08:21,800 --> 00:08:24,580
For example, some foods can induce an iodine deficiency,

222
00:08:24,780 --> 00:08:28,140
et cetera, which then creates a focal nodular goiter

223
00:08:28,140 --> 00:08:29,860
within the thyroid gland.

224
00:08:30,140 --> 00:08:31,460
This is usually non-toxic,

225
00:08:31,560 --> 00:08:32,980
meaning that the patients are euthyroid.

226
00:08:32,980 --> 00:08:36,480
However, regions can rarely become TSH independent,

227
00:08:36,660 --> 00:08:37,880
especially on board exams,

228
00:08:38,220 --> 00:08:40,780
and that would then result in something called a toxic goiter,

229
00:08:40,980 --> 00:08:44,980
where the thyroid gland begins to secrete excess thyroid hormone,

230
00:08:45,180 --> 00:08:47,860
but not under the control of TSH.

231
00:08:48,500 --> 00:08:50,400
Here's a picture of a multinodular goiter.

232
00:08:50,700 --> 00:08:53,060
What you see here is you've got enlargement of the thyroid gland,

233
00:08:53,320 --> 00:08:56,800
but you can see that it's composed of a bunch of these nodules.

234
00:08:58,460 --> 00:09:00,740
Hypothyroidism refers to low thyroid hormone.

235
00:09:00,740 --> 00:09:05,820
This can occur in neonates or it can occur in children and older adults.

236
00:09:06,340 --> 00:09:09,240
When it occurs in neonates or infants, it's called creatinism.

237
00:09:10,020 --> 00:09:12,280
Creatinism refers to a constellation of findings

238
00:09:12,280 --> 00:09:14,620
in which the children have mental retardation.

239
00:09:14,940 --> 00:09:18,040
Remember that thyroid hormone is necessary for normal brain development.

240
00:09:18,440 --> 00:09:20,940
They also get short stature with skeletal abnormalities,

241
00:09:21,340 --> 00:09:25,160
and that's because thyroid hormone is also necessary for normal skeletal development.

242
00:09:25,580 --> 00:09:29,360
They can have coarse facial features, an enlarged tongue, an umbilical hernia.

243
00:09:29,360 --> 00:09:33,820
These are some classic findings in a creatine child.

244
00:09:34,440 --> 00:09:37,880
Now, the causes would be maternal hypothyroidism during early pregnancy.

245
00:09:38,040 --> 00:09:40,980
Of course, the fetus then would not see enough thyroid hormone.

246
00:09:41,580 --> 00:09:42,520
Thyroid agenesis.

247
00:09:42,640 --> 00:09:44,820
If the infant does not form a thyroid gland,

248
00:09:44,900 --> 00:09:47,280
that could result in hypothyroidism during infancy.

249
00:09:48,640 --> 00:09:49,440
Dishormonogenetic goiter.

250
00:09:49,700 --> 00:09:53,400
This is when patients have deficiency in the ability to produce thyroid hormone.

251
00:09:53,400 --> 00:09:58,080
High yield for board exams, the most common enzyme that's deficient

252
00:09:58,080 --> 00:09:59,920
is going to be thyroid peroxidase.

253
00:10:00,660 --> 00:10:05,720
And remember that peroxidase is important for oxidation, organification, and coupling.

254
00:10:06,140 --> 00:10:08,340
And so, of course, you wouldn't be able to produce thyroid hormone,

255
00:10:08,600 --> 00:10:12,180
which would then again result in creatinism in the neonate.

256
00:10:12,180 --> 00:10:14,140
And finally, iodine deficiency.

257
00:10:14,300 --> 00:10:18,140
If the infant doesn't have access to iodine, that could also result in creatinism.

258
00:10:19,440 --> 00:10:22,480
Myxedema is hypothyroidism in an older child or adult.

259
00:10:23,040 --> 00:10:24,960
Clinical features here include myxedema.

260
00:10:25,540 --> 00:10:29,100
Myxedema, again, refers to the fact that the tissue appears edematous.

261
00:10:29,200 --> 00:10:32,600
However, instead of having fluid, it has a dough-like consistency to it.

262
00:10:32,920 --> 00:10:36,660
And that's due to an increase in glycosaminoglycans in the skin and the soft tissues,

263
00:10:37,060 --> 00:10:41,280
especially in the larynx, giving them a deepening of their voice and in the tongue.

264
00:10:41,360 --> 00:10:43,960
And those are two classic areas for board examinations.

265
00:10:44,360 --> 00:10:47,440
Again, the larynx giving deepening of the voice and an enlarged tongue.

266
00:10:47,880 --> 00:10:51,860
Now, the patients who have hypothyroidism are going to have a decreased basal metabolic rate

267
00:10:51,860 --> 00:10:53,500
and decreased sympathetic activity.

268
00:10:53,500 --> 00:10:56,520
So they get weight gain despite a normal appetite.

269
00:10:57,100 --> 00:11:02,660
They get slowing of mental activity, muscle weakness, cold intolerance with decreased sweating.

270
00:11:02,800 --> 00:11:04,220
Again, they're burning less ATP.

271
00:11:04,220 --> 00:11:06,960
And they can have bradycardia with decreased output.

272
00:11:07,320 --> 00:11:11,280
The decreased cardiac output could result in shortness of breath and fatigue.

273
00:11:11,700 --> 00:11:17,200
The patients get oligomenorrhea, hypercholesterolemia, so that's important, and constipation.

274
00:11:17,280 --> 00:11:18,960
It's as if the gut slows down.

275
00:11:19,440 --> 00:11:20,280
Now, what are some causes?

276
00:11:20,440 --> 00:11:21,620
They include iodine deficiency.

277
00:11:21,740 --> 00:11:23,780
If you don't have iodine, you can't make thyroid hormone.

278
00:11:24,320 --> 00:11:25,380
Hashimoto's thyroiditis.

279
00:11:25,460 --> 00:11:29,760
This is the most common cause of hypothyroidism in regions of the world where iodine is sufficient.

280
00:11:30,220 --> 00:11:33,000
And basically, it's an autoimmune destruction of the thyroid gland.

281
00:11:33,000 --> 00:11:36,400
Some drugs, like lithium, can cause damage to the thyroid.

282
00:11:36,760 --> 00:11:40,740
And finally, if the thyroid was removed surgically or there was radioablation, for example,

283
00:11:40,820 --> 00:11:44,780
if the patient had Graves' disease and you radioablated the thyroid, that could then result,

284
00:11:44,900 --> 00:11:49,800
of course, in hypothyroidism, assuming that the patient was not taking thyroid replacement hormone.

285
00:11:50,340 --> 00:11:51,700
Next topic is thyroiditis.

286
00:11:51,780 --> 00:11:54,000
The first of these is Hashimoto's thyroiditis.

287
00:11:54,120 --> 00:11:56,600
This is where you have autoimmune destruction of the thyroid gland.

288
00:11:56,940 --> 00:11:59,180
It's associated with HLA-DR5.

289
00:11:59,260 --> 00:12:00,420
That's particularly high yield.

290
00:12:00,420 --> 00:12:05,380
Again, this is the most common cause of hypothyroidism, where iodine levels are sufficient.

291
00:12:05,900 --> 00:12:09,840
Clinical features are that it can initially present with hyperthyroidism, but eventually

292
00:12:09,840 --> 00:12:11,600
progresses to hypothyroidism.

293
00:12:11,900 --> 00:12:15,040
Now, the idea here is that let's pretend this is a graph of time.

294
00:12:15,480 --> 00:12:17,500
Let's pretend here the patient is normal thyroid.

295
00:12:17,920 --> 00:12:21,040
Now, when they get Hashimoto's, what's going to first happen is they're going to destroy

296
00:12:21,040 --> 00:12:23,360
the current thyroid follicles.

297
00:12:23,360 --> 00:12:27,700
And as they do that, some of the thyroid hormone will actually leak out into the blood, resulting

298
00:12:27,700 --> 00:12:30,020
in an initial hyperthyroidism.

299
00:12:30,380 --> 00:12:34,440
Now, as they continue to destroy the thyroid, then the amount of thyroid hormone will decrease,

300
00:12:34,580 --> 00:12:35,160
decrease, decrease.

301
00:12:35,620 --> 00:12:39,540
And so they may eventually come back to a point where they're euthyroid, but actually they're

302
00:12:39,540 --> 00:12:41,760
on their way down and they're becoming hypothyroid.

303
00:12:42,180 --> 00:12:44,260
And so eventually they'll become hypothyroid.

304
00:12:44,780 --> 00:12:45,860
And that's what I say here.

305
00:12:45,980 --> 00:12:50,360
It can initially present as hyperthyroidism, but eventually progresses to hypothyroidism.

306
00:12:50,360 --> 00:12:55,460
When that occurs, there'll be a decrease in T4, and of course that would result in an increase

307
00:12:55,460 --> 00:12:56,080
in TSH.

308
00:12:56,460 --> 00:13:01,920
Because remember that T4 controls a number of TRH receptors on the anterior pituitary.

309
00:13:02,580 --> 00:13:06,800
Again, recall that when the T4 goes down, the TSH goes up, and that's because if this

310
00:13:06,800 --> 00:13:13,040
is the anterior pituitary, it's these thyroid-releasing hormone receptors that are going to be increased

311
00:13:13,040 --> 00:13:14,780
when there is less T4.

312
00:13:14,780 --> 00:13:20,380
And again, that would result in an increased production of TSH by the anterior pituitary.

313
00:13:21,180 --> 00:13:26,200
Often present in the patient's serum are antithyroglobulin and antimicrosomal antibodies.

314
00:13:26,720 --> 00:13:28,820
These are signs of thyroid damage.

315
00:13:28,980 --> 00:13:30,320
They do not mediate the disease.

316
00:13:30,440 --> 00:13:32,840
Rather, they are markers that the thyroid has been damaged.

317
00:13:33,240 --> 00:13:35,780
Here is a histologic image from a patient with Hashimoto's thyroiditis.

318
00:13:36,640 --> 00:13:40,900
Notice that there's an abundant amount of chronic inflammation, and that focally you

319
00:13:40,900 --> 00:13:42,340
get formation of germinal centers.

320
00:13:42,420 --> 00:13:43,880
That's particularly high yield.

321
00:13:43,880 --> 00:13:47,120
You need to know that you get chronic inflammation with formation of germinal centers.

322
00:13:47,720 --> 00:13:52,740
And in addition, some of the cells that normally surround the thyroid follicle are turning pink.

323
00:13:52,820 --> 00:13:57,300
And so you can see that there's this increased pink color to the cytoplasm of these cells,

324
00:13:57,440 --> 00:13:59,380
and that's called herthal cell change.

325
00:13:59,700 --> 00:14:03,580
And so these patients get chronic inflammation with germinal centers and herthal cells.

326
00:14:04,060 --> 00:14:06,960
Now, I'd also like to remind you that patients that have Hashimoto's thyroiditis,

327
00:14:07,080 --> 00:14:08,920
they have an increased risk for B-cell lymphoma.

328
00:14:09,260 --> 00:14:11,320
Again, the idea here is you've got these germinal centers,

329
00:14:11,320 --> 00:14:14,740
so you're also going to be generating post-germinal center B-cells,

330
00:14:14,740 --> 00:14:17,320
which would eventually form something called the marginal zone.

331
00:14:17,600 --> 00:14:20,120
Recall this from the white blood cell lectures.

332
00:14:20,520 --> 00:14:22,840
And so you can get a marginal zone lymphoma,

333
00:14:23,000 --> 00:14:25,700
and that, again, is a complication of Hashimoto's thyroiditis.

334
00:14:26,040 --> 00:14:29,100
And so if you've got a patient with long-standing Hashimoto's on the board exam,

335
00:14:29,100 --> 00:14:33,260
and they present with an enlarging thyroid gland, think about a B-cell lymphoma.

336
00:14:34,200 --> 00:14:36,600
Subacute, decorvains, granulomatous thyroiditis.

337
00:14:36,720 --> 00:14:38,100
Some people call this subacute.

338
00:14:38,220 --> 00:14:39,100
Some call it granulomatous.

339
00:14:39,860 --> 00:14:40,840
Some call it decorvains.

340
00:14:40,920 --> 00:14:42,700
Just put all the words together and make it easy.

341
00:14:43,160 --> 00:14:45,100
Subacute, decorvains, granulomatous thyroiditis.

342
00:14:46,060 --> 00:14:49,340
Again, subacute implies that it occurs after an acute process,

343
00:14:49,440 --> 00:14:52,280
and so this actually occurs after a viral infection.

344
00:14:52,680 --> 00:14:55,540
The granulomatous tells you that you're going to get granulomatous inflammation.

345
00:14:55,540 --> 00:14:59,800
Now, this inflammation can present with transient hyperthyroidism.

346
00:15:00,040 --> 00:15:02,580
However, it's limited, and it does not progress.

347
00:15:02,620 --> 00:15:04,740
It does not progress to hypothyroidism.

348
00:15:05,040 --> 00:15:08,560
Perhaps the most high-yield point on this slide is that they have a tender thyroid.

349
00:15:09,020 --> 00:15:11,740
If a young woman comes in and has a tender thyroid,

350
00:15:12,020 --> 00:15:14,180
this would be one of the key things on your differential.

351
00:15:14,620 --> 00:15:16,120
And so this is particularly unique.

352
00:15:16,280 --> 00:15:18,780
We haven't really covered anything where we had a tender thyroid.

353
00:15:19,160 --> 00:15:20,520
And so, again, tender thyroid.

354
00:15:20,760 --> 00:15:24,340
Think about subacute decorvain, granulomatous thyroiditis.

355
00:15:24,980 --> 00:15:26,700
Rydell's fibrosing thyroiditis.

356
00:15:26,940 --> 00:15:30,580
This is when you get chronic inflammation with extensive fibrosis of the thyroid.

357
00:15:31,000 --> 00:15:33,240
It presents as hypothyroidism.

358
00:15:33,440 --> 00:15:36,060
But more importantly, especially for board exams,

359
00:15:36,160 --> 00:15:39,260
they have a hard-as-wood, non-tender thyroid.

360
00:15:39,760 --> 00:15:42,140
Hard-as-wood thyroid is sort of the buzz phrase here.

361
00:15:42,340 --> 00:15:45,340
In that case, you think about Rydell's fibrosing thyroiditis.

362
00:15:45,760 --> 00:15:48,420
Now, it's very important to note, especially for board examinations,

363
00:15:48,420 --> 00:15:51,920
that these patients get fibrosis that not only involves a thyroid,

364
00:15:51,920 --> 00:15:55,080
but can extend to local structures, for example, the airway.

365
00:15:55,480 --> 00:15:59,500
And so the classic history on a board examination would be that the patient has a hard-as-wood thyroid

366
00:15:59,500 --> 00:16:01,740
and also has involvement of local structures.

367
00:16:02,240 --> 00:16:06,760
Now, when you have a history of a firm thyroid with involvement of local structures,

368
00:16:06,900 --> 00:16:09,800
you would really, one of the other things to think about would be carcinoma.

369
00:16:10,160 --> 00:16:12,160
And in particular, there is a type of carcinoma,

370
00:16:12,240 --> 00:16:15,020
which we're going to cover in a moment, called anaplastic carcinoma.

371
00:16:15,520 --> 00:16:20,460
Anaplastic carcinoma often spreads outside of the thyroid and invades, for example, the airway.

372
00:16:20,460 --> 00:16:25,420
However, anaplastic carcinoma of the thyroid is a disease of older people,

373
00:16:25,780 --> 00:16:29,320
whereas Rydell's fibrosing thyroiditis is classically a young female.

374
00:16:29,720 --> 00:16:31,420
So this is very high yield to note.

375
00:16:31,760 --> 00:16:36,620
If you've got a thyroid lesion that involves local structures in a young patient,

376
00:16:36,980 --> 00:16:39,280
you're thinking about Rydell's fibrosing thyroiditis.

377
00:16:39,400 --> 00:16:43,540
If you had the same clinical history, but the patient was 70 or 80, for example,

378
00:16:43,920 --> 00:16:46,240
then you'd be thinking about anaplastic carcinoma.

379
00:16:46,580 --> 00:16:49,100
Of course, you could do a biopsy to prove this.

380
00:16:49,100 --> 00:16:52,820
However, on board exams, this is a particular high yield distinction to be made.

381
00:16:53,400 --> 00:16:56,740
Thyroid neoplasia refers to a tumor formation within the thyroid.

382
00:16:57,120 --> 00:17:00,440
This classically presents as a distinct solitary nodule.

383
00:17:00,900 --> 00:17:04,300
Thyroid nodules are much more likely to be benign than they are malignant.

384
00:17:04,740 --> 00:17:07,220
When a patient has a thyroid nodule that you're worried about,

385
00:17:07,280 --> 00:17:09,420
you can do a radioactive uptake study.

386
00:17:09,800 --> 00:17:12,160
When you do a iodine radioactive uptake study,

387
00:17:12,160 --> 00:17:16,800
you would see increased uptake in, for example, Graves' disease or nodular goiter,

388
00:17:17,160 --> 00:17:20,920
whereas you would have decreased uptake in adenomas and carcinomas.

389
00:17:21,300 --> 00:17:24,200
Now, when there's decreased uptake, that would then warrant a biopsy.

390
00:17:24,540 --> 00:17:26,300
Very high yield to note for board exams.

391
00:17:26,740 --> 00:17:29,640
The key mechanism by which you biopsy the thyroid is FNA.

392
00:17:30,180 --> 00:17:33,260
Examiners like to ask you, what's the mechanism of sampling the thyroid?

393
00:17:33,260 --> 00:17:35,660
FNA, fine needle aspiration.

394
00:17:36,160 --> 00:17:39,180
You don't want to biopsy the thyroid because it's a very bloody organ,

395
00:17:39,560 --> 00:17:41,760
and when you biopsy it, it will bleed like crazy.

396
00:17:42,120 --> 00:17:45,260
So instead, you simply stick a needle in, and you pull out some cells,

397
00:17:45,340 --> 00:17:47,020
and you analyze them under the microscope.

398
00:17:47,740 --> 00:17:51,160
The first of the neoplastic disorders is follicular adenoma.

399
00:17:51,300 --> 00:17:53,840
This is a benign tumor of follicular glands.

400
00:17:54,400 --> 00:17:56,240
Now, one of the very high yield points to note

401
00:17:56,240 --> 00:17:59,460
is that these glands will be surrounded by a fibrous capsule.

402
00:17:59,940 --> 00:18:01,460
I'll show you a picture of that in a moment.

403
00:18:03,260 --> 00:18:05,480
But rarely it can secrete thyroid hormone.

404
00:18:05,720 --> 00:18:07,260
Here's a picture of a follicular adenoma.

405
00:18:07,400 --> 00:18:08,520
Here's the normal thyroid.

406
00:18:08,720 --> 00:18:10,480
Notice that it's made up of all of these follicles

407
00:18:10,480 --> 00:18:12,360
with this pink material called colloid.

408
00:18:12,880 --> 00:18:14,640
And here is the tumor.

409
00:18:15,260 --> 00:18:17,860
This area here that I'm marking with a T is the tumor.

410
00:18:18,300 --> 00:18:21,740
Now, of course, the tumor is also making follicles,

411
00:18:21,900 --> 00:18:23,680
hence the term follicular adenoma.

412
00:18:24,220 --> 00:18:27,040
However, notice that there's this dense pink capsule

413
00:18:27,040 --> 00:18:30,260
clearly demarcating this tumor from the rest of the thyroid gland.

414
00:18:30,260 --> 00:18:33,060
And again, that's the hallmark of follicular adenoma.

415
00:18:33,260 --> 00:18:35,760
You've got creation of all these follicle-like spaces,

416
00:18:36,060 --> 00:18:38,420
but it's clearly surrounded by a dense capsule.

417
00:18:39,040 --> 00:18:40,940
Moving on to carcinoma, there's four types,

418
00:18:41,020 --> 00:18:43,640
and you need to spend a couple moments to burn this into your mind.

419
00:18:44,060 --> 00:18:46,140
Papillary, follicular, medullary, anaplastic.

420
00:18:46,240 --> 00:18:48,320
It's worth sort of repeating this over and over again

421
00:18:48,320 --> 00:18:49,340
until you memorize it.

422
00:18:49,800 --> 00:18:52,700
Papillary, follicular, medullary, and anaplastic.

423
00:18:53,280 --> 00:18:56,700
And so papillary carcinoma is the most common type of thyroid carcinoma.

424
00:18:57,040 --> 00:18:59,720
Represents 80% of thyroid carcinoma.

425
00:18:59,720 --> 00:19:03,900
Very high yield to note that exposure to ionizing radiation in childhood

426
00:19:03,900 --> 00:19:05,000
is a major risk factor.

427
00:19:05,420 --> 00:19:09,600
Classic example would be a child who was radiated for severe acne.

428
00:19:09,820 --> 00:19:11,420
And so if you radiate the face,

429
00:19:11,500 --> 00:19:15,440
which was one of the treatments used for severe acne decades ago,

430
00:19:15,640 --> 00:19:17,280
then you'd also be radiating the neck,

431
00:19:17,580 --> 00:19:19,640
which means that you'd be radiating the thyroid.

432
00:19:20,000 --> 00:19:21,120
Again, very high yield.

433
00:19:21,440 --> 00:19:24,480
Ionizing radiation increases the risk for papillary carcinoma.

434
00:19:24,480 --> 00:19:27,140
Here's a histologic image of papillary carcinoma.

435
00:19:27,560 --> 00:19:33,760
And classically, papillary carcinoma will form papillary finger-like projections.

436
00:19:34,260 --> 00:19:36,960
However, what's important to note is that the nuclear features

437
00:19:36,960 --> 00:19:38,960
are what define papillary carcinoma.

438
00:19:39,420 --> 00:19:41,200
And so what you would classically see in the nucleus

439
00:19:41,200 --> 00:19:43,600
is something called orphan anti-eyed nuclei.

440
00:19:44,160 --> 00:19:46,220
And so the idea here is that if this is the nucleus,

441
00:19:46,400 --> 00:19:49,940
notice that there's this white clearing in the center of the nucleus,

442
00:19:49,940 --> 00:19:52,160
and these are called orphan anti-eyed nuclei

443
00:19:52,160 --> 00:19:55,620
because you get this white clearing in the center of the nucleus.

444
00:19:56,140 --> 00:19:59,220
In addition, another feature will be the presence of nuclear grooves.

445
00:19:59,620 --> 00:20:01,660
Can you see that in this nucleus, for example,

446
00:20:01,740 --> 00:20:04,540
there's this line or this groove within the nucleus?

447
00:20:04,940 --> 00:20:07,020
In addition, for example, here in this nucleus,

448
00:20:07,180 --> 00:20:09,880
notice that there's this line or this groove within this nucleus.

449
00:20:10,420 --> 00:20:11,920
And so those are called nuclear grooves,

450
00:20:11,980 --> 00:20:15,020
and they are also a sign of papillary carcinoma.

451
00:20:15,440 --> 00:20:17,780
Now, because you classically get papillary architecture,

452
00:20:17,780 --> 00:20:20,680
you can get calcification of that papillary architecture,

453
00:20:20,860 --> 00:20:22,740
which would then result in a simoma body.

454
00:20:23,160 --> 00:20:26,040
Again, a simoma body is a concentric, layered calcification,

455
00:20:26,200 --> 00:20:28,540
and so you can kind of see the circles of calcification.

456
00:20:28,700 --> 00:20:32,140
Especially notice here, you can clearly see the layering of the calcium

457
00:20:32,140 --> 00:20:34,940
in this particular part of the simoma body.

458
00:20:35,480 --> 00:20:37,720
Now, papillary carcinoma classically spreads

459
00:20:37,720 --> 00:20:39,480
to the cervical nodes within the neck.

460
00:20:39,760 --> 00:20:41,680
However, even when it spreads to the lymph nodes,

461
00:20:41,740 --> 00:20:43,420
it has an excellent prognosis.

462
00:20:44,060 --> 00:20:47,220
Follicular carcinoma is a malignant proliferation of follicles.

463
00:20:47,220 --> 00:20:49,640
Now, similar to a follicular adenoma,

464
00:20:49,800 --> 00:20:51,880
it would be surrounded by a fibrous capsule.

465
00:20:52,280 --> 00:20:55,680
However, the follicles will invade through the capsule.

466
00:20:56,020 --> 00:20:57,220
Very high yield to note.

467
00:20:57,620 --> 00:21:00,600
In follicular adenoma and follicular carcinoma,

468
00:21:00,880 --> 00:21:03,260
you have a tumor of follicles

469
00:21:03,260 --> 00:21:05,560
that is surrounded by a fibrous capsule.

470
00:21:05,920 --> 00:21:07,880
However, in follicular carcinoma,

471
00:21:08,140 --> 00:21:10,340
the cells will invade through the capsule,

472
00:21:10,520 --> 00:21:12,680
and that's the hallmark of follicular carcinoma.

473
00:21:13,020 --> 00:21:14,900
And board examiners love to go after that.

474
00:21:14,900 --> 00:21:17,780
And so here's a picture of a thyroid just showing you what we mean.

475
00:21:18,060 --> 00:21:20,380
You can clearly see that there's this dense capsule

476
00:21:20,380 --> 00:21:21,400
surrounding this tumor,

477
00:21:21,480 --> 00:21:23,080
and the capsule should have extended here.

478
00:21:23,460 --> 00:21:26,480
But notice that the tumor has extended here beyond the capsule.

479
00:21:26,900 --> 00:21:29,400
And so the spread of cancer beyond the capsule

480
00:21:29,400 --> 00:21:32,360
indicates that you have a follicular carcinoma.

481
00:21:33,040 --> 00:21:34,300
Now, I would like to use this image

482
00:21:34,300 --> 00:21:36,760
to highlight one more high yield board point.

483
00:21:37,020 --> 00:21:39,220
And that is, if you were to stick a needle into this thyroid

484
00:21:39,220 --> 00:21:40,320
and pull it out,

485
00:21:40,320 --> 00:21:43,780
what you would pull out are follicular cells or follicle-like cells,

486
00:21:44,120 --> 00:21:45,900
you wouldn't really be able to assess

487
00:21:45,900 --> 00:21:47,320
what's going on with the capsule.

488
00:21:47,900 --> 00:21:50,680
And so therefore, you need to know that FNA biopsy

489
00:21:50,680 --> 00:21:52,340
or fine needle aspiration biopsy

490
00:21:52,340 --> 00:21:55,100
cannot distinguish between follicular adenoma

491
00:21:55,100 --> 00:21:56,340
and follicular carcinoma.

492
00:21:56,680 --> 00:21:58,720
And that's because the only way to make that distinction

493
00:21:58,720 --> 00:21:59,820
would be to see the capsule.

494
00:22:00,180 --> 00:22:01,020
And to see the capsule,

495
00:22:01,120 --> 00:22:02,900
I would either need to see the growth specimen

496
00:22:02,900 --> 00:22:05,680
or I might need to examine the entire capsule

497
00:22:05,680 --> 00:22:06,440
under the microscope

498
00:22:06,440 --> 00:22:08,920
to make sure that there is no microscopic invasion.

499
00:22:09,400 --> 00:22:10,940
But again, very high yield to note

500
00:22:10,940 --> 00:22:12,640
for the purposes of board examinations

501
00:22:12,640 --> 00:22:14,920
that FNA, fine needle aspiration,

502
00:22:15,100 --> 00:22:16,260
cannot make the distinction

503
00:22:16,260 --> 00:22:18,840
between follicular adenoma and follicular carcinoma.

504
00:22:19,300 --> 00:22:20,800
Another very high yield principle

505
00:22:20,800 --> 00:22:21,960
with follicular carcinoma

506
00:22:21,960 --> 00:22:24,520
is that follicular carcinoma likes to go to the blood

507
00:22:24,520 --> 00:22:26,340
and it spreads hematogenously.

508
00:22:26,680 --> 00:22:27,880
Now, that's a little bit of a deviation

509
00:22:27,880 --> 00:22:28,920
from a general principle

510
00:22:28,920 --> 00:22:30,940
because remember that the general principle

511
00:22:30,940 --> 00:22:33,320
is that carcinoma likes to spread to lymph node.

512
00:22:33,640 --> 00:22:34,980
However, this is a carcinoma

513
00:22:34,980 --> 00:22:36,080
that likes to go to blood.

514
00:22:36,440 --> 00:22:37,900
Remember that there are four carcinomas

515
00:22:37,900 --> 00:22:39,220
that like to do that classically

516
00:22:39,220 --> 00:22:40,440
for board exam purposes.

517
00:22:40,920 --> 00:22:42,940
Renal cell carcinoma likes to go to the renal vein

518
00:22:42,940 --> 00:22:43,840
and spread to the blood.

519
00:22:44,600 --> 00:22:46,880
Hepatocellular carcinoma likes to go to the hepatic vein

520
00:22:46,880 --> 00:22:47,740
and spread to the blood.

521
00:22:48,660 --> 00:22:50,720
Follicular carcinoma likes to go into the blood.

522
00:22:51,060 --> 00:22:53,460
And chorial carcinoma very quickly goes into the blood.

523
00:22:53,580 --> 00:22:55,000
And so these are the four carcinomas

524
00:22:55,000 --> 00:22:56,340
that are the key exceptions.

525
00:22:56,620 --> 00:22:58,640
We talked about that in the neoplasia chapter.

526
00:22:58,960 --> 00:23:01,680
Medullary carcinoma is a malignant proliferation of C cells.

527
00:23:01,680 --> 00:23:05,060
The C cells normally sit adjacent to the follicles

528
00:23:05,060 --> 00:23:05,920
within the thyroid.

529
00:23:06,380 --> 00:23:09,480
This tumor is associated with high levels of calcitonin.

530
00:23:09,820 --> 00:23:12,300
And that's because the C cells normally make calcitonin.

531
00:23:12,640 --> 00:23:15,280
Now remember that calcitonin can lower the calcium

532
00:23:15,280 --> 00:23:16,800
by causing renal excretion.

533
00:23:17,120 --> 00:23:19,560
However, at physiologic levels, it's normally inactive.

534
00:23:19,960 --> 00:23:22,240
But with these high levels produced by the tumor,

535
00:23:22,560 --> 00:23:24,580
you can actually get hypocalcemia.

536
00:23:25,100 --> 00:23:27,440
In addition, because you're producing so much calcitonin,

537
00:23:27,700 --> 00:23:29,580
some of that calcitonin can actually deposit

538
00:23:29,580 --> 00:23:31,020
within the tumor as amyloid.

539
00:23:31,300 --> 00:23:34,340
And this would be an example of localized amyloidosis.

540
00:23:34,420 --> 00:23:36,880
And in fact, I covered this in our amyloid lectures.

541
00:23:37,300 --> 00:23:38,400
And so here's a classic picture.

542
00:23:38,520 --> 00:23:41,200
And the high yield is that you have these malignant tumor cells

543
00:23:41,200 --> 00:23:43,100
within this amyloid stroma.

544
00:23:43,200 --> 00:23:45,960
Notice all this pink material here is all amyloid.

545
00:23:46,420 --> 00:23:48,160
And so again, if you did a fine neal aspiration

546
00:23:48,160 --> 00:23:49,740
and you pulled out a little bit of tissue,

547
00:23:50,060 --> 00:23:53,460
you would see malignant cells in an amyloid stroma.

548
00:23:53,620 --> 00:23:54,860
Very high yield description.

549
00:23:55,420 --> 00:23:57,440
Malignant cells in an amyloid stroma.

550
00:23:58,000 --> 00:23:59,480
Now, it's also high yield to note

551
00:23:59,480 --> 00:24:01,180
that there are some familial cases

552
00:24:01,180 --> 00:24:03,020
of medullary carcinoma of the thyroid.

553
00:24:03,420 --> 00:24:06,420
These are associated with MEN2A and MEN2B.

554
00:24:06,800 --> 00:24:09,460
Now, MEN refers to multiple endocrine neoplasia,

555
00:24:09,520 --> 00:24:11,360
and this is when you get neoplasia

556
00:24:11,360 --> 00:24:12,940
within multiple endocrine organs.

557
00:24:13,320 --> 00:24:16,460
In MEN2A, you get medullary carcinoma of the thyroid,

558
00:24:16,720 --> 00:24:17,920
which we're talking about here,

559
00:24:18,360 --> 00:24:20,540
pheochromocytoma, which we'll talk about later,

560
00:24:20,860 --> 00:24:22,340
and parathyroid adenomas,

561
00:24:22,440 --> 00:24:23,720
which we'll also talk about later.

562
00:24:24,240 --> 00:24:27,040
In MEN2B, you get medullary carcinoma of the thyroid,

563
00:24:27,520 --> 00:24:30,220
pheochromocytoma, and ganglioneuromas,

564
00:24:30,360 --> 00:24:31,940
particularly of the oral mucosa.

565
00:24:32,440 --> 00:24:34,580
Now, in both of these, MEN2A and 2B,

566
00:24:34,900 --> 00:24:37,200
you're gonna get medullary carcinoma of the thyroid,

567
00:24:37,400 --> 00:24:39,040
and that really is the key cancer

568
00:24:39,040 --> 00:24:40,140
that these patients get.

569
00:24:40,560 --> 00:24:42,060
Now, what's very high yield to note,

570
00:24:42,200 --> 00:24:44,200
particularly for the purpose of board exam,

571
00:24:44,460 --> 00:24:46,720
is that when patients have familial cases

572
00:24:46,720 --> 00:24:48,560
of medullary carcinoma of the thyroid,

573
00:24:48,880 --> 00:24:51,060
it's classically associated with a mutation

574
00:24:51,060 --> 00:24:52,240
in the RET oncogene.

575
00:24:52,240 --> 00:24:54,420
Now, this is important because if you suspect

576
00:24:54,420 --> 00:24:57,720
a familial predisposition to medullary carcinoma of the thyroid,

577
00:24:58,200 --> 00:25:00,340
you can test the patients for RET mutation.

578
00:25:00,760 --> 00:25:01,940
If there is a RET mutation,

579
00:25:02,240 --> 00:25:05,320
the patient can elect to remove the thyroid prophylactically

580
00:25:05,320 --> 00:25:08,140
to remove the risk of medullary carcinoma of the thyroid.

581
00:25:08,560 --> 00:25:11,840
Very high yield because it has major clinical implications.

582
00:25:12,540 --> 00:25:14,380
So, if there is a RET mutation

583
00:25:14,380 --> 00:25:16,560
and you're suspecting a familial case

584
00:25:16,560 --> 00:25:18,180
of medullary carcinoma of the thyroid,

585
00:25:18,180 --> 00:25:20,960
that will then lead to prophylactic thyroidectomy.

586
00:25:21,280 --> 00:25:23,400
The last carcinoma is anaplastic carcinoma.

587
00:25:23,560 --> 00:25:26,660
Remember, papillary, follicular, medullary, anaplastic.

588
00:25:27,160 --> 00:25:29,820
Anaplastic is an undifferentiated malignant tumor

589
00:25:29,820 --> 00:25:32,140
of the thyroid, classically seen in the elderly.

590
00:25:32,560 --> 00:25:34,180
Now, this cancer is highly malignant,

591
00:25:34,340 --> 00:25:35,900
and so it invades local structures

592
00:25:35,900 --> 00:25:38,900
and can lead to dysphagia or respiratory compromise.

593
00:25:39,460 --> 00:25:41,040
Now, this would lead to a clinical scenario

594
00:25:41,040 --> 00:25:44,140
that is very similar to Rydell's fibrosing thyroiditis.

595
00:25:44,460 --> 00:25:47,060
However, remember that in Rydell's fibrosing thyroiditis,

596
00:25:47,060 --> 00:25:48,660
it would be in a younger patient,

597
00:25:48,780 --> 00:25:50,020
classically a younger female,

598
00:25:50,500 --> 00:25:52,520
whereas in anaplastic carcinoma,

599
00:25:52,620 --> 00:25:53,820
you're thinking about the elderly.

600
00:25:54,380 --> 00:25:56,160
In addition, of course, you could do a biopsy.

601
00:25:56,460 --> 00:25:57,500
In anaplastic carcinoma,

602
00:25:57,600 --> 00:25:59,420
you would see these highly malignant cells

603
00:25:59,420 --> 00:26:01,460
that don't resemble anything

604
00:26:01,460 --> 00:26:04,020
that would normally be present within the thyroid,

605
00:26:04,360 --> 00:26:06,120
and that's what the word anaplastic means,

606
00:26:06,380 --> 00:26:08,720
that you've got a very undifferentiated tumor

607
00:26:08,720 --> 00:26:11,120
that's not really forming any normal structures.

608
00:26:11,980 --> 00:26:12,800
And with that point,

609
00:26:12,800 --> 00:26:15,320
we conclude our discussion of the thyroid gland.

